<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ghiyasuddin Azam Shah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 1390–<a href="/years/1411">1411</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ghiyasuddin Azam Shah was sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> from about 1390 to 1411, the third ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilyas-shahi-dynasty">Ilyas Shahi dynasty</a>. His reign was prosperous and peaceful. He exchanged embassies with the Ming emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, corresponded with the Persian poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hafez">Hafez</a>, and endowed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>. He is remembered in Bengal for his justice, and Bengali literature flourished at his court.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ghiyasuddin-azam-shah.jpg" alt="Ghiyasuddin Azam Shah" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sourav Das from Santa Barbara, USA · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tomb_of_Ghiyasuddin_Azam_Shah,_Narayanganj,_Bangladesh.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
